deep.reconstruction:
Context: section 2 of a manuscript on moduli of elliptic curves in characteristic not 2. The section works out the finite combinatorics behind the structures on the 2-torsion that the moduli problem needs. The next page passes to the 2-Sylow subgroups of S_4. This page continues the previous page's dictionary.

Previous page: a pair (V, line in V*) with V a rank-2 F_2-vector space amounts to a 2-element set C with V = F_2^C. The extra data then becomes an F_2^C-torsor S. Such a torsor is the same as a family of F_2-torsors (A_c), c in C, that is, a degree-2 cover A -> C with A of cardinality 4. Then S = prod_c A_c is the set of sections of A over C, which embeds S into P_2(A).

This page states the dual picture. Put D = S / (diagonal translation), a 2-element set. Then S -> D is a degree-2 cover, A is an F_2^D-torsor, and A = prod_{d in D} S_d (the sections of S over D). This gives A embedded in P_2(S), together with the incidence relation between S and A.

Check: with A_c = {a, sa} and A_c' = {a', sa'}, the four sections {a,a'}, {a,sa'}, {sa,sa'}, {sa,a'} form the edges of a 4-cycle on the vertex set A. So:
- A = vertices of a square;
- S = edges of the square;
- C = A/involution = pairs of opposite vertices (the diagonals);
- D = S/involution = pairs of opposite edges;
- the third 2-element set (omega) = the two orientations of the square.
Each vertex lies on two edges, which gives A embedded in P_2(S). Vertex/edge duality explains the symmetric 'Dualement'.

The automorphism group of the whole structure is the wreath product Z/2 wr Z/2 = D_4 (order 8), a 2-Sylow subgroup of S_4 = Aut(A). The three 2-element sets C, D, omega are the torsors attached to the three nonzero characters of D_4^ab = F_2^2. This is why the next page writes omega ∧ C ∧ D ≅ trivial (the sum of the three characters is zero), together with omega_S ≅ C, omega_A ≅ D and C ≅ ∧_d S_d, D ≅ ∧_c A_c.

The 'Formulaire' lists the data: three 2-element sets C, D, omega, plus two 4-element sets S -> D and A -> C with D = S/≃_S and C = A/≃_A. Its isomorphisms are given on the next page.
